<!-- 10 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-answer">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">10</span><h2>The Answer &amp; Three Sanity Checks</h2></div>

  <div class="vcard v-grn" style="margin-bottom:1rem">
    <span class="vtag">Answer</span>
    <div class="eq">\[ FS_&#123;\text&#123;long term&#125;&#125; \approx 1.9 \]</div>
    <p class="small">Modified Bishop, converged, using 20+ slices. With the 10 slices tabulated above you get 1.887; refining to 20 gives 1.906. Quote it as <strong>1.9</strong> — the geometry is read off a drawing and the phreatic surface is approximate, so three decimals would be false precision.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Never report a factor of safety without testing it. Three checks, each taking seconds:</p>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-teal">
      <span class="vtag">Check 1 &mdash; against OMS</span>
      <h4>1.79 vs 1.90</h4>
      <p>The Ordinary Method gives 1.79 &mdash; about <strong>6&ndash;7% lower</strong>. Bishop should always come out higher, by roughly 5&ndash;20%. If yours came out <em>lower</em>, the iteration or a sign is wrong.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-blu">
      <span class="vtag">Check 2 &mdash; slice count</span>
      <h4>10 → 20 → 40</h4>
      <p>1.887, 1.906, 1.901. Changes in the second decimal only, so the answer has converged. If it were still marching in one direction you would need more slices.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-rust">
      <span class="vtag">Check 3 &mdash; turn the water off</span>
      <h4>1.90 → 2.14</h4>
      <p>Dry, the same slope gives 2.14. Water costs <strong>11%</strong> of the factor of safety. It <em>must</em> move in that direction, and by a plausible amount.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>And one thing this answer is not.</strong> FS = 1.9 belongs to <em>this circle only</em>. The question handed us the failure surface; a real analysis would search hundreds of circles and report the lowest. That is what the <a href="/civil/cee340/slope-stability#sec-calc2">critical circle search</a> in the slope stability chapter does. Never present a single-circle result as "the" factor of safety of a slope.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 11 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-takeaway">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">11</span><h2>What to Carry Away</h2></div>

  <div class="qref">
    <div><span>"Long term"</span><span>drained &rarr; c', &phi;', subtract u</span></div>
    <div><span>Geometry first</span><span>coordinates before any soil mechanics</span></div>
    <div><span>Depth check</span><span>which layers does the arc actually cut?</span></div>
    <div><span>Weight</span><span>everything above the base, layer by layer</span></div>
    <div><span>Strength</span><span>from the soil at the base only</span></div>
    <div><span>&alpha; sign</span><span>(x<sub>m</sub> &minus; x<sub>O</sub>)/R; toe slices resist</span></div>
    <div><span>Denominator</span><span>&Sigma;W sin&alpha;, no iteration needed</span></div>
    <div><span>m<sub>&alpha;</sub></span><span>cos&alpha; + sin&alpha;tan&phi;'/FS &mdash; the implicit bit</span></div>
    <div><span>First guess</span><span>use the OMS answer</span></div>
    <div><span>Convergence</span><span>3&ndash;4 passes is normal</span></div>
    <div><span>Bishop vs OMS</span><span>5&ndash;20% higher, always</span></div>
    <div><span>One circle</span><span>is not the slope's FS</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>The Method in One Sentence</span>
    <p>Cut the sliding mass into strips, work out what gravity asks of each strip and what its base can supply, add both columns up, and — because the strength term secretly depends on the answer — guess, recompute, and repeat until the number stops moving.</p>
  </div>
</section>

